{
  "$schema": "../_schema/producers.schema.json",
  "kind": "producers",
  "source": "claude",
  "notes": "Where goods are made (design §4, geographic supply, designer decision). A settlement makes a good at the sum of the output levels (1–3, at most 3) of every rule whose conditions all hold; nearPoi counts every site in its domain. A market's supply of a good is the best producer within reach, at its output less a level per 3 steady days of road (SUPPLY_DAYS_PER_LEVEL), down to −2 (scarce) when none is near enough. Common goods (food, fuel, timber, stone) have many producers; specialised goods (metal, gear, arms, armour, healing, alchemicals, arcana) come from fewer towns and cities; luxuries only from the great harbours and the capitals; relics barely anywhere (the antiquities dealers of the greatest cities): most come out of dungeons and ruins as loot. The label names the producer when the market explains a price.",
  "entries": [
